========================================
ABSOLUTE POSITIONING – MY EXAMPLE
========================================


----------------------------------------
WHAT I APPLIED
----------------------------------------

.box2{
    position: absolute;
}

No top, left, right, or bottom values
were given.


----------------------------------------
WHAT HAPPENS FIRST
----------------------------------------

When position: absolute is applied:

- .box2 is removed from normal flow
- It no longer occupies space
- Other elements behave as if it does not exist


----------------------------------------
WHY BOX3 MOVED UP
----------------------------------------

Since .box2 is removed from flow:

- The browser ignores its space
- .box3 shifts upward
- .box3 takes the place where .box2 originally was


----------------------------------------
WHY BOX2 LOOKS LIKE IT DID NOT MOVE
----------------------------------------

Even though .box2 is removed from flow:

- No top or left values were given
- So the browser keeps it at its original
  static position visually

It appears like it stayed in the same place,
but actually it is floating.


----------------------------------------
WHY OVERLAPPING HAPPENS
----------------------------------------

Now two elements occupy the same vertical area:

1) .box3 moved up
2) .box2 is floating at its old position

Since .box2 comes later in the HTML:

- It is painted above .box3
- Overlapping occurs

So the yellow box overlaps the magenta box.


----------------------------------------
POSITIONING REFERENCE IN THIS EXAMPLE
----------------------------------------

The parent <div> has:

    position: static; (default)

Since no ancestor has:

    position: relative;
    position: absolute;
    position: fixed;
    position: sticky;

.box2 uses the initial containing block
(viewport/body) as reference.

However, since no top/left is given,
it remains at its original visual position.


----------------------------------------
FINAL RESULT
----------------------------------------

- .box2 is removed from normal flow
- .box3 moves upward
- .box2 stays visually in the same place
- Overlapping happens
- This is the expected behavior
  of absolute positioning
